WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.013 --> 00:00:11.430
VICTOR SZALVAY:
Hello, and welcome

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.650
to our talk on
Google Cloud Golden

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.490
Path to Application Delivery.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.680
My name is Victor Szalvay.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.290
I'm a product manager, and
I work in the DevOps space

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.490
at Google Cloud.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.120
I'm joined by my
colleague, Chris Willis.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.870
CHRIS WILLIS: Hi, my
name is Chris Willis,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.210
and I'm a customer
engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.330
I'm really focused on
helping our customers succeed

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.690
with Kubernetes, and helping
people build and run cloud

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.220
native applications at scale.

00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:38.430
VICTOR SZALVAY:
So Chris and I are

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.340
going to talk to you all
today about application

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.840
development with the
specific focus on Kubernetes.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.300
So we're going to look at
developer tools and CI/CD,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.850
which are often a pain
point for organizations,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.030
but really are also an
essential part of the journey.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.670
I think many of you know
the benefits of CI/CD.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.790
But just to kind of
recap quickly, it

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.890
increases developer
productivity.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.760
And that's directly linked
to customer value generation.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.830
And getting that
value out to customers

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.410
faster means you're
more competitive.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.360
You're scoring higher
customer satisfaction

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.490
with your customers.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.310
And with better
automation and pipeline

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.950
parity comes higher
quality and security.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.860
But we understand that you
are where you are, right?

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.410
And CI/CD and
DevOps improvements

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.680
need to happen incrementally
and with your existing tools

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.910
and processes.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.810
So they say that proof
is in the pudding, right?

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.170
And our research highlights that
elite performers, the ones who

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.510
ship code multiple
times per day,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.450
are 1.8 times more likely
to achieve or exceed

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.310
their commercial
goals, including

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.330
things like profitability,
productivity, and customer

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.090
satisfaction.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.020
But our research also shows that
organizations are increasingly

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.330
successful at adopting CI/CD.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.310
So more and more of
you out there are

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:04.060
going the path of CI/CD, and are
seeing those improved business

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.520
outcomes as a result.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.420
You may be here on your
CI/CD journey, right?

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.760
Developers are
iterating and committing

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.420
code, which then feeds
into a CI/CD pipeline.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.370
And ultimately, end
products are delivered out

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.480
to your customers.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.480
But you're also faced
with some challenges.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.810
And for some of you, that
includes self-managing

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.120
your CI/CD infrastructure.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.440
Also bespoke and complicated
configuration pipelines.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.230
There's often weak separation
of duties and concerns

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.630
when it comes to CI/CD systems.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.113
And often those CI/CD
systems are overused.

00:02:43.113 --> 00:02:45.030
They're doing build and
test, but they're also

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.070
doing the kitchen sink.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.160
And lastly, there are
variances between developer

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.080
and deployment
environments, which

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.620
surfaces unwelcome
surprises in production.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.240
So this ultimately
leads to FUD, right?

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.690
And from the operations
side of the house,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.670
we're talking about
fear of deployment.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.670
Are developers really
iterating in production

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.900
like Kubernetes environments?

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.290
Are they even
adopting Kubernetes?

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.470
And when they are,
is the innovation

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.020
they're doing around rendering
tools affecting things

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.420
like pipeline reliability?

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.283
Chris?

00:03:23.283 --> 00:03:24.700
CHRIS WILLIS: Yeah,
this is really

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.500
why I jumped at the
opportunity to get

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.407
involved with the golden path.

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.490
So some of the customers
I've worked with struggle

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.340
to get their developers to work
directly with Kubernetes, just

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:36.130
because they have their
development workflow.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:39.460
They have their local Docker
container deployments.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.220
And they're used to testing
against that, and just kind

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.240
of working like that.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.300
And it's really something
that they're not

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.620
interested in changing.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.320
And it makes sense, because
operators were really

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.090
sold on Kubernetes when
it exploded in popularity.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.630
But in some cases, developers
haven't really followed suit.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.240
They really weren't in the room
when that jump to Kubernetes

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:01.960
was made.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.700
And they're kind
of now just being

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.385
told to deal with
it, essentially.

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:09.760
VICTOR SZALVAY:
Yeah, and I think

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.270
there's also impact for the
security side of the house,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.200
right?

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.089
A lot of uncertainty there.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.040
Is there enough
automation to go around

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.140
for things like security
analysis and vetting

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.390
before things go out the door?

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:26.860
Or are we relying on manual
processes for that vetting?

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:31.090
And also, are we finding
these security issues too late

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.390
in the development cycle,
causing a lot of churn?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.740
So there's a lot of FUD.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.040
And what we wanted to
do here was present

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.520
a pathway that's been
built out and derived

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.120
from a lot of the learnings
that we've taken at Google

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:47.420
internally.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.320
So we identified the
following three concepts.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.050
And as I mentioned, these
have helped our developers

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:58.400
stay productive internally while
enabling automation at scale.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.330
So the first one is to
decouple and insulate

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.660
from configuration change.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.260
So Kubernetes
manifest rendering,

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.410
it's often tightly coupled
with CI/CD pipelines.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.500
And that really creates sort
of almost like a gridlock

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.210
situation, where both
sides, developers working

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.050
in their rendering
workflows and operations

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.350
with their CI/CD pipelines,
they're hesitant to make moves.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.350
They don't want to break
each other, so to speak.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.400
And so instead, we want to
separate out configuration.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.840
And we want to separate out
the responsibility model.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.420
Next is unification
of the dev experience.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.440
And what I mean here is across
the developer inner loop,

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.470
the CI/CD loop, but
also all the way

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.780
out to production
Kubernetes clusters.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.870
The biggest pain developers
face in Kubernetes development

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.700
is knowing the
quirks and variances

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.948
across the various loops.

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:57.990
So in the inner loop you
might be using something

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.700
like minikube.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.210
In CI/CD there may be some
slightly different environment.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.440
And ultimately, your production,
enterprise Kubernetes

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.810
environments, those might
be different altogether.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.060
And I'm talking about
things like the use

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.000
of different
environmental variables,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.090
perhaps different secrets,
replicas, and so on.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.890
So what I really want is a
way to bridge these variances

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:26.160
and provide a more
seamless experience

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.820
to all my developers.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.730
And the last point
here is shifting left.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:37.530
It wastes a lot of developer
energy and nerves, frankly,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.510
when policy or
security constraints

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.980
are discovered downstream.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.710
So what we want to do is
actually inject those policy

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:48.250
checks right into the IDE to
catch those issues before devs

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:52.770
spend too much time
down a certain pathway.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:56.210
So we're excited to
share with you today

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.820
the notion of the golden
path for application

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.020
delivery on Google Cloud.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.980
Now the key to the golden path
is a product called Scaffold.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.950
Scaffold is an open source
command line utility

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.890
that unifies the
developer experience

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:16.240
with build, deploy,
test, security,

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.420
and provides a uniform
experience across all

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.730
of those loops.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.670
You can use Scaffold
with your existing tools.

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.400
But it's also, of course,
bundled with the Google Cloud

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.440
tools you see here.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.020
And while you may be familiar
with Cloud Code and Cloud

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.960
Build, we're really
excited to introduce

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.770
Cloud Deploy, our fully
managed, opinionated, continuous

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.370
delivery service purpose
built with Google Kubernetes

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:44.840
Engine in mind.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.275
Chris, you have any
thoughts to add on this?

00:07:47.275 --> 00:07:48.650
CHRIS WILLIS:
Yeah, just I wanted

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:49.950
to highlight a few things here.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:53.660
So for those of you that are
not familiar with Cloud Code,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.870
it's an extension of VS
Code in [? Inteligase, ?]

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:02.570
or it is an extension that
is produced for these IDEs.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:05.090
So there's no real
switching between IDEs.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.060
You don't have to change
your IDE just because you're

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:08.940
using Cloud Code.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.260
It's baked right into your IDE.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.600
The Cloud Code run
on Kubernetes feature

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.720
really clearly delineates where
the developer's responsibility

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.000
ends and ops begins.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.760
This means developers can really
focus on writing code, and not

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.400
trading YAML files, and
troubleshooting surprises

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:26.970
when deploying to staging.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.270
So this is really that
shift left mentality

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.700
that Victor was talking about.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:35.240
And then integration
with Scaffold,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.370
it being an open
source project means

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.100
ops can really start developing
an opinionated approach

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.179
to CI/CD.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:43.730
I know with the
introduction of Kubernetes,

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:46.370
which is really a platform
to build platforms--

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.350
it's not a platform itself--

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:52.460
there's a lack of opinion
on Build and Deploy.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:56.540
So Scaffold can really help
you build those opinions,

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.620
and carry them to
other cloud vendors.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.890
So you're not locked
into Google Cloud.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.260
I mean, obviously Google
Cloud integrates heavily

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:04.640
with Scaffold.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.890
And we're going to make that
a first class experience.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:09.530
But you can take the
Scaffold container image

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.330
and run that same Scaffold
configuration elsewhere.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.368
So you're not really
locked into our services.

00:09:15.368 --> 00:09:17.410
VICTOR SZALVAY: Yeah, and
Chris, at the beginning

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.930
we talked a lot about
incremental adoption and sort

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:24.520
of the reality of making
changes to your CI/CD pipelines.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:27.310
We want to encourage all
of you to really start

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.200
with your most urgent need.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.260
And for many of our
customers, they've

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.240
got a development loop going.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.470
They've got CI going.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.270
But CD is the next frontier.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:40.630
And so perhaps you
can get the benefits

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.940
of Scaffold and the golden
path by adopting something

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:46.840
like Cloud Deploy as a
fantastic starting point.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.710
Or perhaps you can
start with Cloud Code.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.930
As Chris mentioned,
this is an IDE plug-in

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:54.400
which bundles Scaffold.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.950
And it provides a
graphical user experience

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.440
on top of the Scaffold CLI.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.332
Chris, anything else you
want to add about Cloud Code?

00:10:03.332 --> 00:10:05.790
CHRIS WILLIS: Yes, I mean, the
coolest thing about this one

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:06.610
is that it's free.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:09.660
So developers and operators
can, right after this session,

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:11.520
go grab Cloud Code.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.710
And there's no dependency
on Google Cloud.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:17.078
We have sample apps that'll
just work out of the box.

00:10:17.078 --> 00:10:18.870
You'll click the Run
on Kubernetes feature,

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.450
and bam, it's
deployed to minikube.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:26.190
So it's a really cool, powerful
tool that hopefully y'all will

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.075
check out after this.

00:10:29.075 --> 00:10:30.700
VICTOR SZALVAY: Yeah,
and the last one,

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:32.218
here is perhaps Cloud Build.

00:10:32.218 --> 00:10:33.760
If you're looking
for a fully managed

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.180
build and continuous
integration platform,

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.480
Cloud Build offers
a Scaffold builder,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.050
which easily integrates
Scaffold into your continuous

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.290
integration workflow.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.520
So Chris, I think the best way
to experience the golden path

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:51.630
is to see it in action.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.900
And what do you think?

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.820
Should we get into the demo?

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.190
CHRIS WILLIS: Yeah, let's do it.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.540
VICTOR SZALVAY: All
right, here we go.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.500
All right, so I just have a
basic Go application here.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.450
And we're going to
use Scaffold in it

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:09.690
to generate our deployment
manifests, our Kubernetes

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:14.720
deployment manifests,
and our Scaffold YAML.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.870
And these are both going to
be applied against minikube.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:21.080
We'll add a test
here real quick.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:24.080
But I want to break
down this Scaffold YAML.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.080
So we have a build
step which is actually

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.760
going to build our image.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.800
And we're using
Buildpacks because we're

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:31.880
going from source.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.370
You'll notice there's
no Docker file here.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.560
And a test step,
which is just going

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:40.580
to run a simple Go test
inside the container image.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:45.020
And a deployment manifest,
where our Kubernetes deployment

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:46.130
is defined.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:47.390
And this is all on minikube.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:50.870
So it's just going to pull
whatever Docker image resolves

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:52.955
there for Go mod image.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.770
All right, so now
we're going to use

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.020
that Run on Kubernetes
button that we

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.030
had talked about previously.

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:06.260
And this is going to execute
the Scaffold YAML steps,

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:10.580
and use the deployment
manifest that we generated

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:14.500
to deploy to minikube.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.560
And we'll go and check
out our application.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:21.560
Just a very simple
Go application.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:25.510
Refresh it in the
counter increments.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.640
So now we want to make a
change to our application

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.230
to have that quick
feedback as a developer.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:34.533
So we want to make a local
change and see if it works.

00:12:34.533 --> 00:12:36.950
So we're just going to change
this color from blue to red.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.905
I know, nothing huge.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:45.160
And we see file synced
as soon as we saved it.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.920
Scaffold picks up on
that change locally.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:49.220
And here we go.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.990
We have a red instead
of a blue application.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:54.700
Very important.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.440
All right, let's stop it here.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.780
And obviously,
debugging is important.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.700
So this is my favorite feature.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:06.390
So let's go down and
insert a breakpoint.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:08.610
Looks like we had one left over.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:12.250
But insert a
breakpoint at line 81,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.320
right where the
counter increments.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.620
And click Debug on Kubernetes
this time instead of Run

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:18.970
on Kubernetes.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:21.310
So this will actually
inject a debugger

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.580
into the container and
forward those events.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.260
This is all part of Scaffold.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.780
Forward those events to the IDE.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.490
And so when we
refresh this page,

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:34.520
it's going to drop us right
into the debug output.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:39.970
And we can search through
local variables here.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.610
And we can go into the
call stack and look

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:46.210
at all sorts of things,
troubleshoot certain things

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:51.160
about our application that
might have gone wrong.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:52.360
We can resume.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:55.300
We can step over, all
the normal debug stuff.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:57.940
So very important
for developers to be

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.220
able to debug
their applications.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:03.160
So right now we have
this dev inner loop.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:05.230
The dev is working
through Cloud Code,

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:07.960
deploying locally
using minikube.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.030
They have their
application deployed,

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.070
and they're working away at it.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:15.490
So now the ops folks are
going to go and create

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.780
a Cloud Build trigger.

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:21.100
Which any time there is
a PR pulled into master,

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:22.750
or some change to
master, this is

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:27.040
going to fire off and
execute those scaffold steps

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.710
that we specified.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:36.410
And so now we have any time
the developer pushes to get

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.780
or PR is approved, Cloud
Build will trigger.

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.820
All right, so you'll see
we've added some things

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.620
to our Scaffold manifest.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.570
We have a profile
named staging, which

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:52.620
will deploy the deployment
staging manifest,

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:54.830
which is we just copied
the deployment manifest

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:58.610
that we generated earlier
with Scaffold in it, and added

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:00.590
whatever is specific to staging.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.842
And we did the exact
same for Canary and Prod.

00:15:02.842 --> 00:15:04.550
And this is very
important, because these

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:10.190
are going to stretch across
into what Cloud Deploy is later

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:14.968
going to use to deploy to the
various staging Canary and Prod

00:15:14.968 --> 00:15:15.510
environments.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:17.150
They're kind of
like scopes, right?

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:22.550
You're trying to get across
how this tool should behave.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:25.130
Or how this deployment
should behave

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:26.630
in a different
environment, which

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:31.250
is where we talked about kind
of the applying of opinions,

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:34.190
and making an opinionated
CI/CD process that

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:37.995
sticks with Scaffold and not
the Cloud providers' specific

00:15:37.995 --> 00:15:38.495
tools.

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:46.160
We have our pipeline YAML, where
we're specifically saying what

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:47.660
the stages of the pipeline are.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.300
So we have staging
Canary and Prod.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:54.278
And for those stages in
the pipeline-- so each one

00:15:54.278 --> 00:15:55.820
in the Cloud Deploy
interface, you'll

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:59.510
see it'll create kind of a box
as it visualizes the pipeline.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:01.760
For each one you're
saying, I want

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:05.220
you to use this Scaffold
profile called staging.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:09.110
So this is where
Scaffold and Cloud Deploy

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:13.820
meet is that you're specifying
a specific step in the pipeline

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.250
and what Scaffold
profile should run.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.930
Then we have the actual
configuration for the staging

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:25.310
step in the pipeline.

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:28.730
So remember, in the
last file we specified

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:31.670
that there was a step in
the pipeline called staging.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.310
This is where we're going
to say, OK, staging.

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:39.290
You're going to use this Google
Cloud project, this cluster

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:41.390
name, called staging,
and then obviously

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:42.770
the region that it's in.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:52.670
Now we're going to create
the pipeline in Cloud Deploy.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:55.400
We are going to
configure the staging

00:16:55.400 --> 00:17:00.800
step in Cloud Deploy using
those files we just discussed.

00:17:04.244 --> 00:17:06.119
And then we're going to
trigger the pipeline.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:20.200
All right, so we go to
the Cloud Build interface,

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:25.329
and we will check the
build triggering here.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:31.420
Going to go and do
all of those Scaffold

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:33.040
steps that we've defined.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:42.010
And we'll go into Cloud Deploy.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.630
Check out the pipeline.

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:48.800
This will trigger and
do the actual deploy,

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.980
as we've specified.

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:54.830
And we'll go and check
on our application.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.600
And here it is, running.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:01.430
So the developer is now
working in their inner loop.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:03.140
They submit a PR.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:04.520
An operator approves it.

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:07.640
Cloud Build triggers
because master was changed.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:12.410
The artifact is built and
stored in artifact registry.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:17.060
And then the associated staging
process in the Cloud Deploy

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:18.950
pipeline is triggered.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:23.290
And a deployment to
staging is initiated.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.770
And we're going to promote that
staging release we deployed.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:29.155
We're going to promote
it to a Canary deploy.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.930
And that will deploy
to Prod, right?

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.313
So normal Canary deployment,
it'll deploy to Prod.

00:18:39.313 --> 00:18:41.730
And then if there's anything
wrong, you just roll it back.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.355
And hopefully
nothing bad happened.

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:55.480
So let's make sure that that
is deployed to the Canary,

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:57.220
or the Canary is
deployed correctly.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:03.690
And let's check on
the application.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:04.480
Looks good.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:06.810
We got our little
chickadee there

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.930
to signify that it
is Canary deployment.

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:21.390
And now we will promote the
release from a Canary deploy

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.858
to Prod.

00:19:23.858 --> 00:19:25.650
And there's going to
be an extra step here.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.230
So see that it's pending.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.360
So we know that we need
an approval, someone

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.720
with the authority to approve
this release to production.

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:37.420
Not just anyone can push
to production, hopefully.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.120
And so this person is
going to come in and say,

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:41.310
OK, yeah, looks good.

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:44.470
We're going to approve
that rollout to production.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:55.670
And so we'll go back
and check on it.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:59.630
And the deployment
to Prod is underway.

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:01.392
It finished.

00:20:01.392 --> 00:20:03.350
Now, let's check on our
production application.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.525
Let's make sure
everything's in order.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:10.250
Looks good.

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:12.800
All right.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:16.270
So our final state is
the developer, again,

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.460
working in Cloud Code,
their local loop,

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.830
which can be minikube.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.295
Local can kind of be anywhere.

00:20:22.295 --> 00:20:23.920
That's what I found
out with customers.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:28.420
It can be I'm on a laptop,
and I'm on an airplane local.

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:31.180
Or it can be I'm just
in my normal GKE cluster

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:32.960
that someone made for me.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.150
And that's where I
do my development.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.510
So they're doing
that dev interloop,

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:39.250
getting that fast feedback.

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:43.120
They submit a PR
to the Git repo.

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:46.030
And an operator approves the PR.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:50.470
And there is a trigger
from Cloud Build

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:52.420
because master has changed.

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:56.800
The artifact is generated,
stored in artifact registry.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:02.080
And the associated staging
pipeline step is run.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:05.960
We obviously had an extra
Canary deployment step in here.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:09.970
And once Canary
tries to go to Prod,

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:12.400
an operator actually has to
come in and approve that.

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:14.590
And then you have
your change to Prod.

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:18.370
So that is the full
end to end picture.

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:22.140
And that is the end of the demo.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.510
All right, so let's summarize
what we've seen today.

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:27.420
The golden path for application
delivery on Google Cloud

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:29.580
centers around
unifying pipelines

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.400
across dev, CI, and CD.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.560
We accomplished
this with Scaffold.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:36.490
Scaffold is open source.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:41.190
It's a CLI, but it's wrapped
in a UI provided by Cloud Code.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:42.750
It helps developers
get productive

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:45.390
with local Kubernetes
development and debugging

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:47.370
right within the IDE.

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.550
But Scaffold also
creates pipeline parity

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:56.200
by commands that fit right
into your CI and CD processes.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:58.090
You can adopt this
approach incrementally

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:02.938
starting with only those
components that you need most.

00:22:02.938 --> 00:22:04.480
So here are some
resources that we've

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:06.280
talked about in today's talk.

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:07.930
If you'd like to
get started, we'd

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:11.280
invite you to check these out.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.413
Chris?

00:22:13.413 --> 00:22:15.330
CHRIS WILLIS: Yeah, so
just one thing to note.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:19.680
That GitHub link at the bottom
is just on my own GitHub.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:23.880
But that read me and
that starter application

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.200
is exactly what I used
in the demo here today.

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:28.500
So if you want to try
it out on your own,

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:30.600
it'll all work just
following the readme

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:33.930
and generating all of
the YAML files as you go.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:37.993
So feel free to check that out.

00:22:37.993 --> 00:22:39.910
VICTOR SZALVAY: Well,
that's it for a session.

00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:42.850
I want to thank my colleague
Chris for a fantastic demo.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:47.160
And I want to thank you all
for spending some time with us,

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.060
and learning about the golden
path for application delivery

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:52.990
on Google Cloud.

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:54.540
Thanks.

